1
00:00:00,210 --> 00:00:04,670
The next section in your text deals with appendix, and this is a relatively short section,

2
00:00:04,830 --> 00:00:11,070
so we're going to actually do two sections in this video, both appendix and then inflammatory bowel disease.

3
00:00:11,590 --> 00:00:15,250
So the appendix, the only major thing to be aware of is something called acute appendicitis.

4
00:00:15,350 --> 00:00:18,130
Obviously, this is acute inflammation of the appendiceal wall.

5
00:00:18,790 --> 00:00:21,350
This is the most common cause of acute abdomen.

6
00:00:21,350 --> 00:00:28,770
Acute abdomen is when a patient presents with severe abdominal pain, which is often a surgical emergency.

7
00:00:29,310 --> 00:00:34,310
This is related to obstruction of the appendix by lymphoid hyperplasia or a fecalist.

8
00:00:34,410 --> 00:00:42,210
So the idea here is that if you've got the appendix, the way by which the appendix gets inflamed is that you've got to block it with something.

9
00:00:42,590 --> 00:00:47,950
And once you block it with something, then remember the general principle of pathology that when you create this block,

10
00:00:47,950 --> 00:00:50,510
then basically behind the block you're going to get inflammation.

11
00:00:51,190 --> 00:00:57,130
And so when there's a block, you're then going to get inflammation, in particular here, acute inflammation of the appendiceal wall,

12
00:00:57,130 --> 00:01:01,070
which is, again, called appendicitis or acute appendicitis.

13
00:01:01,390 --> 00:01:06,630
So this is what I say here in the text, and what I mean when I say that you've got to block it either with lymphoid hyperplasia.

14
00:01:06,810 --> 00:01:09,230
So there's a ton of lymphoid tissue in the appendix.

15
00:01:09,270 --> 00:01:14,310
If you go back to your normal histology, remember that you've got a lot of lymphoid tissue within the appendix,

16
00:01:14,310 --> 00:01:19,750
and so you can get lymphoid hyperplasia if the child, for example, has some sort of gastrointestinal infection,

17
00:01:20,210 --> 00:01:24,150
which could then result in appendicitis because it would block the lumen, or a fecalist,

18
00:01:24,210 --> 00:01:27,910
which is basically just a piece of fecal material that would be a cause in adults.

19
00:01:28,390 --> 00:01:33,370
Patients with acute appendicitis, they present with periumbilical pain, fever, and nausea.

20
00:01:33,410 --> 00:01:36,070
These are very kind of classic signs and symptoms.

21
00:01:36,070 --> 00:01:42,150
The pain initially is periumbilical, but eventually localizes to the right lower quadrant.

22
00:01:43,130 --> 00:01:47,070
Rupture of the appendix could result in peritonitis, where you would inflame the peritoneum,

23
00:01:47,370 --> 00:01:49,710
and that would present with guarding and rebound tenderness.

24
00:01:49,890 --> 00:01:58,650
Now, guarding refers to the fact that there's so much pain in the abdomen that the patients keep themselves very tight,

25
00:01:58,750 --> 00:02:02,730
and when you press down on the abdomen, you can feel the muscles sort of very tight,

26
00:02:02,730 --> 00:02:05,270
trying to guard against you pressing any deeper.

27
00:02:05,270 --> 00:02:10,370
And then rebound tenderness is when you press down on the abdominal wall and then let go.

28
00:02:10,690 --> 00:02:13,090
It hurts a tremendous amount when you let go.

29
00:02:13,230 --> 00:02:14,510
That's called rebound tenderness.

30
00:02:15,090 --> 00:02:17,850
And, of course, you can develop an abscess around the appendix.

31
00:02:18,130 --> 00:02:19,730
It's a common complication.

32
00:02:19,890 --> 00:02:22,170
That would be called a periappendiceal abscess.

33
00:02:23,650 --> 00:02:28,730
Moving right along to inflammatory bowel disease, this is a chronic relapsing inflammation of the bowel.

34
00:02:29,170 --> 00:02:30,370
We don't know what causes it.

35
00:02:30,370 --> 00:02:34,570
It could possibly be due to an abnormal immune response to enteric flora.

36
00:02:35,270 --> 00:02:42,330
Classically, the presentation is that you get a patient who has recurrent bouts of bloody diarrhea and abdominal pain,

37
00:02:42,370 --> 00:02:44,530
and this recurrent is important, as you'll see.

38
00:02:45,550 --> 00:02:51,190
Usually it's, or more often, it's a young woman, classically in her teens to 30s,

39
00:02:51,250 --> 00:02:54,990
but it can occur later in life, and, of course, males can be afflicted as well.

40
00:02:54,990 --> 00:03:00,550
Now, this is more prevalent in the West, particularly in Caucasians and those of Eastern European Jewish descent.

41
00:03:01,410 --> 00:03:06,490
It's a diagnosis of exclusion, and that's because when you have a patient who's got bloody diarrhea and abdominal pain,

42
00:03:06,730 --> 00:03:12,150
there are a lot of things that would be up on the top of your differential before you would think about inflammatory bowel disease.

43
00:03:12,490 --> 00:03:14,210
For example, infectious causes.

44
00:03:14,390 --> 00:03:15,950
For example, ischemic causes.

45
00:03:15,950 --> 00:03:23,510
And so, really, you've got to exclude a bunch of things before you can raise inflammatory bowel disease up on your differential diagnosis.

46
00:03:24,330 --> 00:03:29,650
Now, there's two types of inflammatory bowel disease, or at least two major types, ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease,

47
00:03:29,850 --> 00:03:34,490
and it's very important to be familiar with each of these and the distinctions between them.

48
00:03:34,490 --> 00:03:43,430
And so, we're going to walk you through this chart for the sake of making sure that you really understand these two disorders.

49
00:03:43,670 --> 00:03:47,710
So, the first disorder is called ulcerative colitis, and the name tells you a lot.

50
00:03:47,810 --> 00:03:57,310
Once you hear the word ulcerative, you know that the extent of the wall that's going to be involved is going to be predominantly mucosal with some mucosal ulceration.

51
00:03:57,850 --> 00:04:02,790
Remember that the word ulcer basically means that you're damaging the mucosa and a little bit of the submucosa.

52
00:04:02,790 --> 00:04:05,130
And so, I think that's easy, given the name.

53
00:04:05,450 --> 00:04:10,610
As far as the location is concerned, this is going to involve the colon, hence the term colitis.

54
00:04:10,950 --> 00:04:16,850
But what's important to note is that it always starts in the rectum, and then it works its way up the colon.

55
00:04:17,170 --> 00:04:21,790
And as it works its way up the colon, it can extend to any level within the colon.

56
00:04:21,930 --> 00:04:25,890
So, it could involve the rectum only, or it could move up and involve the rectum and the sigmoid,

57
00:04:25,950 --> 00:04:31,730
or it could move up and involve the rectum, sigmoid, and the descending colon, etc.

58
00:04:31,730 --> 00:04:36,270
And so, it'll go up the colon and then sort of stop.

59
00:04:36,490 --> 00:04:41,730
And it can stop anywhere along the colon, but the most proximal that it can go,

60
00:04:42,070 --> 00:04:46,830
as it goes backwards from the rectum up, the most proximal that it can go is the cecum.

61
00:04:47,230 --> 00:04:50,270
So, the cecum is sort of the end point, and of course, that's the end point of the colon.

62
00:04:50,410 --> 00:04:52,950
So, this disease can only involve the colon.

63
00:04:53,390 --> 00:04:57,970
Remember also that when it starts in the rectum, it'll involve the entire radius of the tube.

64
00:04:57,970 --> 00:05:02,890
So, it basically just sort of involves the complete surface of the tube wherever it's present,

65
00:05:03,010 --> 00:05:05,130
and will work its way backward to a certain point.

66
00:05:05,570 --> 00:05:09,730
The remainder of the GI tract, from the terminal ileum all the way up, is unaffected.

67
00:05:09,930 --> 00:05:12,190
So, it's really restricted to the colon.

68
00:05:12,730 --> 00:05:15,510
Now, because it's restricted to the colon, and particularly the rectum,

69
00:05:15,590 --> 00:05:17,990
you're going to have left lower quadrant pain.

70
00:05:18,070 --> 00:05:20,030
The left lower quadrant is where the rectum is located,

71
00:05:20,410 --> 00:05:22,470
and of course, these patients are going to have bloody diarrhea.

72
00:05:22,470 --> 00:05:28,450
Now, when you ask yourself, what is the key histologic hallmark of ulcerative colitis,

73
00:05:28,590 --> 00:05:30,990
the answer is that it is a crypt abscess.

74
00:05:31,290 --> 00:05:32,650
So, what is a crypt abscess?

75
00:05:32,730 --> 00:05:35,170
Well, let's go back and remind ourselves of the normal histology.

76
00:05:35,290 --> 00:05:39,590
When you look at the colon, it's a bunch of these sort of test tubes lined up.

77
00:05:39,890 --> 00:05:41,630
That's the way the colon normally looks.

78
00:05:42,490 --> 00:05:44,190
Each of these is called a crypt.

79
00:05:44,910 --> 00:05:50,630
And so, the key histologic hallmark of ulcerative colitis is that you're going to get neutrophils within these crypts.

80
00:05:50,630 --> 00:05:52,270
So, that's called a crypt abscess.

81
00:05:52,390 --> 00:05:55,090
Now, you can see the crypt going up and down as a test tube,

82
00:05:55,390 --> 00:05:59,470
or if you cut the test tube in cross-section, you would see the crypt looking like this.

83
00:05:59,510 --> 00:06:03,730
Of course, these are the epithelial cells, and then it would be loaded with neutrophils in here.

84
00:06:04,110 --> 00:06:10,010
And so, that is a crypt abscess, and that is the histologic hallmark of ulcerative colitis.

85
00:06:10,130 --> 00:06:11,630
And you do need to be familiar with that.

86
00:06:11,970 --> 00:06:14,310
The histologic hallmark is a crypt abscess.

87
00:06:14,350 --> 00:06:17,630
And I'm going to show you some pictures at the very end of this discussion.

88
00:06:18,790 --> 00:06:20,070
Now, what's the gross appearance?

89
00:06:20,490 --> 00:06:24,310
Well, when you look at the colon grossly, you're going to see pseudopolyps.

90
00:06:24,310 --> 00:06:31,030
And pseudopolyps basically represent these numerous bumps that arise on the surface of the colon

91
00:06:31,030 --> 00:06:35,750
as a response to the healing of the ulceration.

92
00:06:36,090 --> 00:06:38,410
So, after you get the ulceration and then you get healing,

93
00:06:38,670 --> 00:06:41,270
then you're going to get these little bumps throughout the surface of the colon,

94
00:06:41,370 --> 00:06:42,650
and those are called pseudopolyps.

95
00:06:43,010 --> 00:06:45,030
At the same time, there will be loss of hostra,

96
00:06:45,030 --> 00:06:51,450
and I'll show you a picture of loss of the hostra, again, at the very end of this section.

97
00:06:51,910 --> 00:06:52,970
Now, what are the complications?

98
00:06:53,170 --> 00:06:55,990
Well, one of the complications is something called toxic megacolon.

99
00:06:56,390 --> 00:06:58,790
This is where you get massive dilation of the colon,

100
00:06:58,790 --> 00:07:03,850
and the patients start to appear toxic where they are febrile, et cetera.

101
00:07:04,650 --> 00:07:11,490
Another complication, and of course, the complication, by the way, of toxic megacolon is risk of rupture.

102
00:07:11,910 --> 00:07:16,850
Another complication, perhaps the most important, the highest yield as far as the complications are concerned,

103
00:07:16,910 --> 00:07:17,690
is carcinoma.

104
00:07:17,690 --> 00:07:23,150
Now, when patients have ulcerative colitis, the feared complication is that they're going to develop dysplasia,

105
00:07:23,270 --> 00:07:25,530
and then after the dysplasia, they're going to get carcinoma.

106
00:07:26,030 --> 00:07:29,270
Now, the risk of carcinoma is based on two major factors.

107
00:07:29,750 --> 00:07:32,650
Number one, the extent of colonic involvement.

108
00:07:32,790 --> 00:07:35,950
So, the more you involve the colon, the more likely you are to get cancer.

109
00:07:36,430 --> 00:07:38,430
And number two, the duration of the disease.

110
00:07:38,730 --> 00:07:43,270
So, it takes about 10 years of disease before you really begin to worry about cancer.

111
00:07:43,270 --> 00:07:47,490
Now, again, this is super important because it affects the management of patients with ulcerative colitis.

112
00:07:47,970 --> 00:07:51,990
So, patients with ulcerative colitis will actually undergo screening colonoscopy,

113
00:07:52,190 --> 00:07:57,250
and the purpose of screening colonoscopy in their case is to discover dysplasia,

114
00:07:57,370 --> 00:08:01,610
because if there was dysplasia, that would indicate that you're one step closer to carcinoma.

115
00:08:02,150 --> 00:08:08,190
And so, what they'll do during the colonoscopy is they'll go in there and just take random biopsies

116
00:08:08,190 --> 00:08:14,670
from areas of disease or normal, what looks normal, and then they'll submit that to the pathologist,

117
00:08:14,670 --> 00:08:19,010
and the pathologist will assess for the presence or absence of dysplasia.

118
00:08:19,630 --> 00:08:23,050
Now, again, it's important to note, and it's high yield for examination purposes,

119
00:08:23,050 --> 00:08:29,930
that the risk is based on two things, the extent of the colonic involvement and the duration of the disease.

120
00:08:30,330 --> 00:08:33,550
So, usually, you don't get dysplasia until about 10 years into the disease.

121
00:08:34,410 --> 00:08:35,650
What are the associations?

122
00:08:35,870 --> 00:08:36,930
There's a couple associations.

123
00:08:37,170 --> 00:08:39,450
One is called primary sclerosing cholangitis.

124
00:08:39,450 --> 00:08:43,330
I'm going to bury that away in the back of your mind, and we'll talk about it later in our lectures.

125
00:08:43,850 --> 00:08:49,170
But one very important one that I won't get a chance to touch on later in another chapter will be the P. Anka.

126
00:08:49,510 --> 00:08:54,470
So, the P. Anka can be positive in ulcerative colitis, and actually, this is high yield for examination purposes.

127
00:08:55,110 --> 00:09:01,830
Now, when you think about the P. Anka, what I'd like to remind you of is that the P. Anka can also be positive in a couple of vasculitities.

128
00:09:01,830 --> 00:09:08,010
For example, when you think about P. Anka, you think about microscopic polyangiitis, you think about Church-Strauss,

129
00:09:08,330 --> 00:09:10,990
and then you should also think about ulcerative colitis.

130
00:09:11,590 --> 00:09:12,870
Again, that's important.

131
00:09:13,170 --> 00:09:20,650
It's a little bit of a complicated discussion because this P. Anka is a little bit different than the P. Anka that you would have seen in the vasculitis,

132
00:09:20,770 --> 00:09:24,150
but it is important to note that these patients can have a P. Anka.

133
00:09:24,150 --> 00:09:31,090
And the final funny thing that likes to show up on exams is that smoking does protect against ulcerative colitis.

134
00:09:31,330 --> 00:09:33,310
So, it's protective against ulcerative colitis.

135
00:09:34,270 --> 00:09:37,630
Now, let's move to the opposite side of this chart and talk about Crohn's disease.

136
00:09:38,170 --> 00:09:45,770
If ulcerative colitis is superficial involving the mucosa and the submucosa, then Crohn's disease is a full thickness inflammation.

137
00:09:46,590 --> 00:09:51,410
And you get, instead of getting ulcers, you get these knife-like fissures.

138
00:09:51,410 --> 00:09:57,170
So, what I mean by that is that if you're looking at the bowel wall, and, of course, you've got a mucosa and then a submucosa,

139
00:09:57,410 --> 00:10:05,690
and then you've got a muscularis propria and a serosa, the way by which this disease sort of damages the wall

140
00:10:05,690 --> 00:10:12,770
is that you get these large knife-like fissures of inflammation going through the entire thickness of the wall.

141
00:10:13,470 --> 00:10:15,130
So, that's important to note.

142
00:10:15,130 --> 00:10:23,030
Now, the location of Crohn's disease, if ulcerative colitis always starts at the rectum and then works its way back up to some part of the colon,

143
00:10:23,390 --> 00:10:26,250
Crohn's disease can be anywhere from the mouth to the anus.

144
00:10:26,570 --> 00:10:29,130
So, from the mouth all the way down to the anus.

145
00:10:29,610 --> 00:10:35,170
And remember, if ulcerative colitis is a continuous lesion that starts at the rectum and works its way all the way up

146
00:10:35,170 --> 00:10:39,590
and then is continuous around the entire tube, Crohn's disease can have skip lesions.

147
00:10:39,590 --> 00:10:44,130
So, it can hit an area and then skip and then hit another area and then skip, and that's classic.

148
00:10:44,670 --> 00:10:50,130
Now, the most common site of Crohn's disease is the terminal ileum, and the least common site is the rectum.

149
00:10:50,370 --> 00:10:51,810
Most common site, terminal ileum.

150
00:10:51,970 --> 00:10:53,330
Least common site, rectum.

151
00:10:53,690 --> 00:11:00,050
So, because the terminal ileum is the most common site, these patients are going to present with right lower quadrant pain,

152
00:11:00,310 --> 00:11:05,710
again, because the ileum is particularly involved, and they get non-bloody diarrhea, classically.

153
00:11:05,710 --> 00:11:12,150
Now, when you think about what you would see histologically, remember that the hallmark of ulcerative colitis was crypt abscess.

154
00:11:12,490 --> 00:11:16,110
Well, the hallmark of Crohn's disease is just going to be lymphocytes with granulomas.

155
00:11:16,270 --> 00:11:17,610
Granulomas is really important.

156
00:11:17,950 --> 00:11:21,030
So, you can see granulomas in Crohn's disease, and that's high yield.

157
00:11:21,610 --> 00:11:26,370
Now, on gross exam, wherever there was damage, you would see cobblestoning of the mucosa,

158
00:11:26,530 --> 00:11:30,370
and cobblestoning of the mucosa, again, represents the healing process,

159
00:11:30,450 --> 00:11:32,950
and I'll show you a picture of that, as that's a very classic finding.

160
00:11:32,950 --> 00:11:35,690
At the same time, you get creeping fat and stricture.

161
00:11:35,790 --> 00:11:36,790
So, let me explain this.

162
00:11:37,170 --> 00:11:41,950
Remember that in Crohn's disease, what you're going to get is you're going to get mucosa,

163
00:11:42,770 --> 00:11:47,030
and then submucosa, and then muscularis propria, and then serosa,

164
00:11:47,190 --> 00:11:50,370
and you're getting a full thickness inflammation through that entire wall.

165
00:11:50,770 --> 00:11:54,150
Now, remember that outside of the serosa, you've got the mesentery hanging here,

166
00:11:54,270 --> 00:11:56,950
and then you've also got adipose tissue along that mesentery.

167
00:11:57,830 --> 00:11:59,910
So, imagine if you've got a full thickness inflammation,

168
00:11:59,910 --> 00:12:02,710
then the next thing that's going to happen is you're going to get healing.

169
00:12:03,110 --> 00:12:05,810
And when you get healing, you're going to get granulation tissue,

170
00:12:05,910 --> 00:12:08,410
because you've knocked out the stem cells, and you're going to get fibrosis.

171
00:12:08,710 --> 00:12:11,770
So, when you get granulation tissue and fibrosis, a couple things are going to happen,

172
00:12:11,870 --> 00:12:15,130
but most importantly, you're going to have cells in here called the myofibroblasts.

173
00:12:15,530 --> 00:12:18,250
Remember that the myofibroblasts are part of granulation tissue,

174
00:12:18,350 --> 00:12:19,690
and they have contractile function.

175
00:12:19,690 --> 00:12:25,630
So, that contractile function will actually cause this wall to contract and become fibrotic.

176
00:12:26,030 --> 00:12:27,210
So, there'll be two consequences.

177
00:12:27,390 --> 00:12:30,790
Number one, as you create this dense fibrosis along the wall,

178
00:12:30,870 --> 00:12:34,010
you're going to narrow the lumen, creating what is called a stricture.

179
00:12:34,370 --> 00:12:38,390
And so, you can actually sharply reduce the caliber of the lumen of this wall.

180
00:12:38,730 --> 00:12:41,110
And number two, as the myofibroblasts are pulling up,

181
00:12:41,170 --> 00:12:42,890
they're also going to pull the fat up with it.

182
00:12:43,410 --> 00:12:47,110
And so, when you pull the fat up, the fat's going to then creep along the wall.

183
00:12:47,110 --> 00:12:52,150
It's going to come closer in to the serosa, and they call that creeping fat.

184
00:12:52,630 --> 00:12:57,830
So, that's what I mean here in your chart when I say that you get creeping fat and strictures.

185
00:12:58,850 --> 00:13:02,150
Now, the strictures result in a classic sign called the string sign on imaging.

186
00:13:02,530 --> 00:13:05,270
And again, I'll show you a picture of that at the very end.

187
00:13:05,930 --> 00:13:08,550
As far as the complications of Crohn's disease are concerned,

188
00:13:08,750 --> 00:13:11,770
the first one is malabsorption with nutritional deficiency.

189
00:13:12,290 --> 00:13:15,390
And the idea here is that you have the potential to damage the small bowel.

190
00:13:15,390 --> 00:13:17,790
The small bowel is essential for absorption.

191
00:13:18,250 --> 00:13:20,530
And so, if there's significant damage to the small bowel,

192
00:13:20,630 --> 00:13:23,030
you'll get malabsorption with nutritional deficiencies.

193
00:13:23,510 --> 00:13:27,470
At the same time, these patients can develop calcium oxalate nephrolithiasis.

194
00:13:27,550 --> 00:13:31,570
And the idea here is that normally you have oxalate in the gut.

195
00:13:31,690 --> 00:13:34,250
However, we don't really absorb very much of that oxalate.

196
00:13:34,530 --> 00:13:36,490
However, if there's inflammation of the bowel,

197
00:13:36,870 --> 00:13:41,190
that then increases the ability of oxalate to come into the blood.

198
00:13:41,190 --> 00:13:44,570
And if oxalate comes into the blood, it can eventually bind with calcium,

199
00:13:44,890 --> 00:13:50,070
especially in the filtrate of the glomerulus, and then result in kidney stones.

200
00:13:50,190 --> 00:13:53,230
And so, there's an increased risk of calcium oxalate nephrolithiasis,

201
00:13:53,310 --> 00:13:55,210
and that is particularly high yield.

202
00:13:55,650 --> 00:13:57,470
The patients can also form fistulas.

203
00:13:58,030 --> 00:14:01,410
Fistulas are connections, abnormal connections between two tubes,

204
00:14:01,470 --> 00:14:04,310
or abnormal connection between a tube and another surface.

205
00:14:04,650 --> 00:14:05,990
So, that would be called a fistula.

206
00:14:05,990 --> 00:14:11,170
And the idea here, again, is that, recall that you've got full thickness inflammation of the wall, correct?

207
00:14:11,570 --> 00:14:14,970
So, when you have full thickness inflammation of the wall, you are weakening the wall.

208
00:14:15,330 --> 00:14:17,030
Now, this wall could potentially rupture,

209
00:14:17,150 --> 00:14:21,550
and imagine if this ruptures that the entire tube can then plug in to another tube.

210
00:14:21,950 --> 00:14:24,710
And that would then be called a fistula.

211
00:14:25,030 --> 00:14:27,830
So, that's important to understand in relatively high yield.

212
00:14:28,350 --> 00:14:32,270
And then, there is a risk for carcinoma, but only if colonic disease is present.

213
00:14:32,270 --> 00:14:36,390
And that's kind of based on the principles that we've already established for ulcerative colitis,

214
00:14:36,550 --> 00:14:40,290
which is the fact that, when you think about the risk for cancer and ulcerative colitis,

215
00:14:40,410 --> 00:14:41,690
remember that there's two factors.

216
00:14:42,050 --> 00:14:44,770
Factor number one being the extent of colonic involvement.

217
00:14:44,970 --> 00:14:47,410
And so, extent of colonic involvement is important.

218
00:14:47,570 --> 00:14:51,490
And in this particular case, in Crohn's disease, if we have colonic involvement,

219
00:14:51,670 --> 00:14:53,710
then there would be a risk for carcinoma.

220
00:14:53,890 --> 00:14:57,990
And if there's no colonic involvement, then, of course, there would be a limited risk,

221
00:14:58,110 --> 00:15:00,250
or almost no risk, for carcinoma.

222
00:15:00,250 --> 00:15:04,010
Now, there are a bunch of associations with Crohn's disease.

223
00:15:04,190 --> 00:15:08,650
They include ankylosing spondylitis, and we'll talk more about ankylosing spondylitis

224
00:15:08,650 --> 00:15:12,290
when we do the musculoskeletal lecture.

225
00:15:12,990 --> 00:15:15,010
Sacroiliitis, which you'll also hear about there.

226
00:15:15,410 --> 00:15:22,070
You can get a migratory polyarthritis, where you get inflammation of joints,

227
00:15:22,170 --> 00:15:26,810
and it involves multiple joints and can move from joint to joint to joint.

228
00:15:26,810 --> 00:15:36,130
Erythema nodosum, which is a skin lesion, and uveitis, which would result in visual disturbances.

229
00:15:36,890 --> 00:15:39,890
And so, these are some of the associations to merely be familiar with.

230
00:15:40,230 --> 00:15:43,690
And the last point is that there's an increased risk for Crohn's disease in smokers.

231
00:15:43,810 --> 00:15:48,870
So, with smoking, you protect against ulcerative colitis, but you increase the risk for Crohn's disease.

232
00:15:49,350 --> 00:15:52,530
Now, what I'd like to do is show you a few pictures, and then we'll close off this section.

233
00:15:52,530 --> 00:15:57,230
And so, the first picture here, this represents a patient with ulcerative colitis.

234
00:15:57,610 --> 00:16:03,390
Now, the idea here is that this is the rectum, and what we're doing is you can see the redness represents the inflammation,

235
00:16:04,050 --> 00:16:08,330
and you can see that you've also lost the folds as a result of that inflammation.

236
00:16:08,630 --> 00:16:10,990
And you'll see this as we get closer to the normal mucosa.

237
00:16:10,990 --> 00:16:12,090
So, here we are.

238
00:16:12,410 --> 00:16:17,410
The way the ulcerative colitis works is that it always starts in the rectum, and then it works its way all the way up,

239
00:16:17,470 --> 00:16:22,570
hitting the entire surface, until it eventually comes to a certain point where it stops.

240
00:16:22,650 --> 00:16:24,210
So, here, it has stopped right here.

241
00:16:24,470 --> 00:16:26,250
And then this represents normal.

242
00:16:26,850 --> 00:16:27,130
All right?

243
00:16:27,190 --> 00:16:31,010
And it, of course, starts in the rectum, works its way backward, paints the entire tube,

244
00:16:31,070 --> 00:16:32,970
and then stops someplace in the colon.

245
00:16:33,470 --> 00:16:35,470
The furthest it could go would be the cecum.

246
00:16:35,530 --> 00:16:37,830
So, it's not allowed to go into the terminal ilium.

247
00:16:37,830 --> 00:16:40,070
And this is, again, ulcerative colitis.

248
00:16:40,310 --> 00:16:46,310
And the redness of the mucosa with the loss of the mucosal folds indicates that we've got ulcerative colitis as well.

249
00:16:46,830 --> 00:16:51,010
Were you to look histologically, you would see the classic hallmark, which is a crypt abscess.

250
00:16:51,330 --> 00:16:55,010
So, what I've illustrated here is that this circular space represents the crypt,

251
00:16:55,390 --> 00:17:00,510
and within this crypt, you've got all of these neutrophils, which represents the crypt abscess.

252
00:17:01,150 --> 00:17:05,870
When you get ulcerative colitis, you get loss of the holstra, which is these folds here,

253
00:17:05,870 --> 00:17:11,770
all of these folds here in the wall, and that results in something called a lead pipe appearance.

254
00:17:11,910 --> 00:17:13,890
So, here is all normal.

255
00:17:14,570 --> 00:17:15,830
You can clearly see the holstra.

256
00:17:16,050 --> 00:17:19,550
And here, you can see that you've lost them, and that's called a lead pipe appearance.

257
00:17:19,550 --> 00:17:22,750
And I think this is a beautiful example of that.

258
00:17:22,750 --> 00:17:27,470
Now, in the case of Crohn's disease, remember that you're going to get full thickness inflammation.

259
00:17:27,790 --> 00:17:32,010
So, it's going to be full thickness from the top of the wall to the bottom of the wall.

260
00:17:32,310 --> 00:17:36,450
And when you get full thickness inflammation, then the complication of that is that when you get healing,

261
00:17:36,770 --> 00:17:43,970
you're going to get fibrosis of this entire thickness, and, of course, that is then going to narrow the lumen of the bowel.

262
00:17:43,970 --> 00:17:50,210
And so, you can see here that this is normal, and all of a sudden, you've come into an area where this is narrow.

263
00:17:50,850 --> 00:17:54,290
And that, again, is an example of a stricture.

264
00:17:54,750 --> 00:17:59,010
Now, at the same time, this image is also highlighting the idea of cobblestoning,

265
00:17:59,190 --> 00:18:03,590
so that when these patients get healing, the healing is represented by cobblestoning.

266
00:18:03,690 --> 00:18:08,970
And so, you can clearly see it looks like a bunch of bricks laid down on the ground or on the street,

267
00:18:08,970 --> 00:18:11,810
and that's the cobblestone appearance.

268
00:18:12,310 --> 00:18:16,190
And at the same time, you can also notice here that some of the fat has been pulled up,

269
00:18:16,510 --> 00:18:20,170
and so one of the features of Crohn's disease would also be creeping of the fat.

270
00:18:20,670 --> 00:18:23,510
Now, if the patient presents with obstruction, you could do barium studies,

271
00:18:23,570 --> 00:18:27,250
and in this particular case, you can see that this is the normal bowel here,

272
00:18:27,410 --> 00:18:30,970
but when you come to this point of the bowel, the barium is barely passing through,

273
00:18:31,050 --> 00:18:35,050
and that's because there's fibrosis here, and there's also fibrosis here.

274
00:18:35,050 --> 00:18:41,030
Now, the inability to pass barium results in just this tiny wisp of barium coming through,

275
00:18:41,410 --> 00:18:42,690
and that's called a string sign.

276
00:18:43,130 --> 00:18:49,090
And that's the last image that highlights the important principles of both ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease.

277
00:18:49,430 --> 00:18:51,610
So, the next section will be our discussion of colon.

